{
  "name": "happy",
  "integration": "opennms-horizon",
  "description": "A full four-endpoint OpenNMS collection at the default /opennms context path: the two experimental v2 interface collections indexed once for the whole estate, the v1 monitored-service list, and the v1 node walk. Every payload keeps the shape of a real OpenNMS ReST capture and replaces only the values - the node envelope follows the server's own v1 nodes.json test resource (count/totalCount/offset plus a 'node' array), the ifservices envelope follows v1/ifservices.json with its hyphenated 'monitored-service' key and its node-by-LABEL reference, and the two v2 envelopes follow OnmsIpInterfaceList/OnmsSnmpInterfaceList ('ipInterface', 'snmpInterface'). The type mismatch OpenNMS really emits is preserved: a node's own id is the JSON STRING \"1\" while the nodeId on every interface referring to it is the NUMBER 1, which is the whole reason the script compares join keys as text. Proves: the v2 index keeps the run off the per-node fallback entirely (no /rest/nodes/{id}/ipinterfaces request is ever made); an inlined snmpInterface delivers a MAC with the address it is bound to; a LAG port reporting the same physAddr as one of its members is pooled rather than emitted twice; a softwareLoopback ifType 24 port is dropped; an SNMP port with a MAC and no IP still contributes an interface; a labelSource of \"A\" keeps the address-shaped node label out of hostnames; an interface hostName that is a copy of its own IP is refused as a name; the reverse-DNS name of the loopback interface OpenNMS monitors on its own server is \"localhost\", and that is refused as a name too rather than being handed to runZero as a merge signal every OpenNMS node would share; 127.0.0.1 and 169.254.0.0/16 never reach an interface; a lastCapsdPoll in 2035 is clamped to now instead of making the platform reject the whole asset; and monitored services are recorded as names because an OpenNMS monitored service carries no port anywhere in the data model.",
